    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters August 17th with a primary objective: weaponizing speed to manufacture runs. **Corbin Carroll** leads the squad with a dominant 74.59 efficiency score. Even with his lineup spot pending, his high base projection combined with an elite park factor makes him our automatic cornerstone for a day where stolen base opportunities are at a premium. **Austin Riley** also commands significant attention with a 66.15 score; while batting 8th is a drag on his value, the matchup against Bailey Ober (xERA 4.81) and the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery create a rare green-light environment for **Austin Riley** to capitalize on potential base-path movement. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** (61.27) overcomes a tough encounter with Luis Castillo by leveraging a favorable park factor and a premium #2 hole assignment.\n\nOur lineup construction remains sensitive to the volatility of daily confirmed lineups, which remain pending across the board. The decision to keep **Mickey Moniak** (45.58) in the utility slot despite a brutal draw against Blake Snell (xERA 2.39) highlights the optimizer's lean toward players who offer even marginal upside over a static bench. **Mickey Moniak** is skating on thin ice today; had we more depth available beyond the mounting injury pile of **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, he would likely be riding the pine to preserve our game caps. **Henry Bolte** (57.18) sits on the cusp of \"Strong\" status, with his leadoff position providing the necessary boost to overcome his neutral matchup against Michael Wacha.\n\nWe continue to monitor the status of **Gabriel Moreno** (59.74), whose potential #3 spot keeps him as our most efficient catcher play today. If official lineups shift these assumed spots, particularly for **Max Muncy** (53.17) and **Zach McKinstry** (49.35), we may see a cascading drop in offensive efficiency. With **Josh Naylor** inactive today and our IL nursing a significant portion 